## 14. Cross-Page Automation Rules

Implement the following relationships throughout the application.

### Task and project automation

- Creating a task associates it with a selected client and platform.
- Completing a task updates project and Dashboard statistics.
- Starting a timer from a task associates the time entry with that project.
- Completing a project can initiate payout-date tracking when the relevant payout event is confirmed.
- Recording a payment reduces the client's outstanding balance.
- Changing a deadline updates relevant alerts.

### Client automation

- A new client created from Tasks, Time Tracker, or Invoices must appear in Contacts.
- Client details should automatically populate relevant invoice and project forms.
- All projects, invoices, time entries, and payments must remain linked to the same client record.

### Time and invoice automation

- Tracked time updates project and client totals.
- Invoicing billable time marks the appropriate time entries as invoiced.
- Partial invoice payments update the remaining invoice balance.
- Invoice balances and client balances update automatically.
- The same billable time cannot be invoiced twice.
- An invoice can include several projects or time entries when appropriate.

### Earnings automation

- Recorded payments update payment history.
- Platform fees affect net earnings calculations.
- Expected payouts remain separate from confirmed payments.
- Exchange-rate updates affect converted display values without rewriting original transaction values.

### Goal automation

- Completed milestones update goal progress.
- Actual recorded earnings can contribute to an income goal.
- Actual tracked hours can contribute to a work-hours goal.
- Actual recorded savings contributions can contribute to a savings goal.

### Budget automation

- Expenses update category totals and monthly budget progress.
- Recurring expenses generate future occurrences according to the selected recurrence rules.
- Subscription reminders are generated from renewal dates.
- Income and expense reports use the underlying transactions rather than duplicated summary records.

### Dashboard automation

- Dashboard metrics update when relevant records change.
- Needs Attention appears only when appropriate.
- Today's tasks, schedules, goals, and deadlines are drawn from their respective modules.
- Seven-day and monthly statistics use clearly defined date ranges.

## 15. User Experience and Interface Design

Create a polished, premium SaaS interface with a modern productivity-focused aesthetic.

### Design direction

Use a clean, professional layout inspired by high-quality project management and financial SaaS applications.

Recommended characteristics:

- Light background with subtle neutral surfaces.
- Dark, readable typography.
- A restrained primary accent color.
- Green for completed or positive financial outcomes.
- Amber for upcoming deadlines and warnings.
- Red for overdue or critical issues.
- Subtle borders and restrained shadows.
- Consistent spacing and alignment.
- Rounded cards used selectively.
- Readable charts and progress indicators.
- Clear icons paired with labels where needed.

Avoid excessive gradients, oversized cards, unnecessary animations, decorative charts, and visual clutter.

### Layout

Desktop:

- Persistent collapsible sidebar.
- Top bar with page title, search, notifications, and profile/settings.
- Main content area with responsive cards and tables.
- Clear page hierarchy and consistent page padding.

Mobile:

- Responsive layouts.
- Compact navigation.
- Forms optimized for touch.
- Tables converted into readable cards where appropriate.
- Timers and quick actions easy to access.
- No horizontal overflow.

### Interaction design

- Use clear primary and secondary actions.
- Open forms in suitable modals, drawers, or dedicated pages.
- Keep forms concise and group related fields.
- Provide useful empty states with relevant actions.
- Show success and error feedback.
- Confirm destructive actions.
- Preserve user-entered information when validation fails.
- Add search, filtering, sorting, and pagination to large lists.
- Support keyboard navigation and accessible labels.
- Ensure readable color contrast and visible focus states.

Do not overload every page with all possible statistics. Display the most important metrics first and place secondary information in detail views.

## 16. SEO and Content Requirements

Optimize the public-facing product website for relevant search intent while keeping the actual management application private and productivity-focused.

If a public landing page is included, use clear, natural copy focused on phrases such as:

- Freelancer management software.
- Freelancer productivity tool.
- Project and task management for freelancers.
- Freelance time tracking.
- Client and invoice management.
- Freelancer earnings tracker.
- Freelancer budget planner.

Include a clear product title, descriptive page metadata, accessible headings, descriptive links, and appropriate structured data where relevant.

Do not keyword-stuff the application interface.

Private dashboard pages, client information, invoices, and financial records must not be indexed by search engines.

## 17. Technical and Functional Expectations

Build a complete, maintainable application with reusable components and a consistent data model.

Required capabilities include:

- Persistent data storage.
- Reliable CRUD operations for all major records.
- Authentication and secure user-specific data isolation if accounts are supported.
- Database-backed relationships between clients, projects, tasks, invoices, and time entries.
- Working charts and calculations.
- PDF invoice generation.
- Excel and PDF report exports.
- Currency-rate integration with an appropriate fallback.
- Reliable timer persistence.
- Configurable reminders and notification preferences.
- Date-aware scheduling.
- Input validation and error handling.
- Responsive desktop and mobile interfaces.
- Accessible forms and controls.
- Clear loading, empty, and error states.

Keep business logic separate from presentation components where practical.

If an external service, API key, database, or notification permission is required, implement the appropriate integration or clearly identify what must be configured. Do not simulate a real integration and claim it is operational.

Use sensible defaults, but allow the user to customize currencies, hourly rates, platform fees, payout assumptions, budget categories, reminders, and billing rules.

## 18. Acceptance Criteria

Before considering the application complete, verify these scenarios:

1. A user creates a client and the client becomes available in every relevant module.
2. A user creates a Fiverr project worth 100 USDT and the configured 20% fee produces an estimated net value of 80 USDT.
3. A user records a partial payment and the outstanding client balance updates correctly.
4. A user starts and stops a timer, navigates away, returns, and still sees the correct recorded duration.
5. A user manually adds time to a project and the billable amount is calculated correctly.
6. A user generates an invoice from tracked time without invoicing the same time twice.
7. A user records a partial invoice payment and the remaining balance updates correctly.
8. A user completes a task and sees the relevant statistics update on Today and Dashboard.
9. A user schedules a reminder and receives an alert at the configured time when the required notification mechanism is available.
10. A task approaching its deadline appears in Needs Attention and disappears from that section when resolved.
11. A completed project can have its expected platform payout tracked without incorrectly marking the money as received.
12. A user creates a monthly budget, records an expense, and sees the correct category and remaining budget values.
13. A recurring subscription produces the appropriate future reminders and stops when cancelled.
14. A user creates a goal with milestones and sees its progress update correctly.
15. A user exports a selected month's data to Excel and PDF and receives files containing the correct filtered data.
16. Dashboard and report totals reconcile with their underlying records and do not double-count invoices, payments, expenses, or time entries.
17. All major forms, buttons, filters, navigation items, and actions work on desktop and mobile.

## 19. Final Build Instruction

Prioritize correctness, usability, and connected functionality over visual complexity.

Build the application in a logical order:

1. Establish the data model and relationships.
2. Implement shared navigation and the global application layout.
3. Build Contacts and project/task management.
4. Implement time tracking and invoicing.
5. Implement scheduling, reminders, and goals.
6. Implement Earnings and Budget.
7. Build the Dashboard and Today page using real application data.
8. Build Reports and exports.
9. Test calculations, cross-page updates, persistence, alerts, and responsive layouts.

The final result should be an intelligent, polished, practical freelancer management platform that helps the user plan each day, complete projects, manage clients, track every billable hour, understand pending payments, monitor earnings, control expenses, and measure long-term progress from one unified system.

Do not stop at a design mockup. Deliver the most complete working implementation supported by the chosen technology stack, and clearly identify any integrations or configuration steps that remain necessary.
